  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nAbove normal heat will push high temperatures and heat indices \nupwards of 100 degrees this afternoon. Therefore, a Heat Advisory \nhas been issued for Corson, Dewey, Stanley, and Jones counties.\nSoutherly winds will strengthen today, gusting up to 30 to 40 miles \nper hour by the afternoon. These strong gusts will mainly be along \nand west of the James River Valley. When combined with the heat over \ncentral and north central South Dakota, fire weather concerns become \nelevated. The Grassland Fire Danger Index will reach Very High \nlevels west of the Missouri River as a result. Afternoon humidity is \nexpected to fall just shy of the 20 percent or lower criteria for a \nRed Flag Warning, so opting for a Special Weather Statement to cover \nthe near-critical fire weather threat this afternoon. Only other \nitem of note today is that wildfire smoke from the Pacific Northwest \nwill continue to move over the region today and into at least \nMonday. Smoke is expected to remain mostly aloft, creating hazy \nskies. Still, near-surface concentrations could be enough to cause \nminor visibility reductions as well as drop air quality to Moderate \nlevels, impacting those sensitive to smoke. \n\nAn upper-level trough will move over the Northern Plains beginning \nMonday. As a result, a cold front will move over the region Monday \ninto early Tuesday. Storms may develop along this front, and storms \nthat do form have the potential to be severe. The main obstacle to \nstorm formation will be a mid-level cap in place. This cap is \nstrongest over central South Dakota and becomes weaker moving to the \nnortheast. Therefore, the area with the greatest chances for severe \nweather will be over northeastern South Dakota and western Minnesota \nMonday evening. There is still some disagreement amongst the model \nsoundings on just how much of a cap will be over the area, with some \nshowing little to none. Look for this to come into further focus \nover the next couple of forecast cycles as the event draws nearer.\nThe main severe threats out of storms that form will be hail and \nwind. On the hail side, CAPE/Shear combo of roughly 1000-2000 J/kg \nand 40-50 knots at 0-6km respectively will be in place. Mid-level \nlapse rates of up to 8 C/km will also be in place, so hail will be a \npotential threat. Decent confidence in the potential wind threat as \nwell due to a fairly strong signal for DCAPE > 1000 J/kg and low-\nlevel lapse rates approaching the dry adiabatic lapse rate where the \ncap has been eroded. At this time, there is a Slight Risk over parts \nof northeastern South Dakota and western Minnesota as well as \n\nAs the cold front passes over north central South Dakota Monday \nafternoon, winds turn northerly and will increase once again. Gusts \nas high 35 to 45 miles per hour will be in place Monday afternoon, \nonce again mainly over central and north central South Dakota. The \nlatest NBM guidance gives a roughly 30 to 50 percent chance for \ngusts to reach above 45 miles per hour Monday afternoon over Corson \nand Dewey counties, so the potential for a Wind Advisory will be \nmonitored with future forecasts. These strong winds and the \ncontinuation of above normal temperatures Monday will once again \ncreate elevated fire weather conditions over central and north \ncentral South Dakota. The Grassland Fire Danger Index will be at \nHigh to Very High levels. \n\nBehind the cold front Tuesday and Wednesday, below normal \ntemperatures will take hold. Highs both of those days will be in the \n70s to low 80s across most of the forecast area, which is about 5-10 \ndegrees below early August normals. The upper-level trough will move \nout of the area by the end of the week, with a broad, weak ridge \nexpected to take hold of the region. This will lead to the likely \nreturn of above normal temperatures, particularly over central and \nnorth central South Dakota.",
